add("Chemical Kinetics", "M", "Half-life of a first-order reaction is 69.3 minutes. The rate constant k is:", ["0.01 min⁻¹", "0.10 min⁻¹", "0.001 min⁻¹", "1.0 min⁻¹"], 0, "For first order reaction, t_1/2 = 0.693 / k => k = 0.693 / 69.3 = 0.01 min⁻¹.")
add("Chemical Kinetics", "H", "For reaction 2 A + B → C, doubling [A] increases rate by factor of 4, while doubling [B] doubles the rate. Rate law is:", ["Rate = k [A] [B]", "Rate = k [A]² [B]", "Rate = k [A] [B]²", "Rate = k [A]² [B]²"], 1, "Rate ∝ [A]^m [B]^n. 2^m = 4 => m = 2. 2^n = 2 => n = 1. Rate law = k [A]² [B]. Overall order = 3.")
add("Chemical Kinetics", "M", "Units of rate constant for a ZERO-order reaction are:", ["s⁻¹", "mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹", "L mol⁻¹ s⁻¹", "L² mol⁻² s⁻¹"], 1, "For zero order, Rate = k [A]⁰ = k. Units of rate = mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹.")
add("Chemical Kinetics", "H", "A first-order reaction is 75% complete in 40 minutes. Its half-life (t_1/2) is:", ["10 min", "20 min", "30 min", "40 min"], 1, "75% completion corresponds to 2 half-lives (100% -> 50% -> 25%). 2 * t_1/2 = 40 min => t_1/2 = 20 min.")
add("Chemical Kinetics", "H", "When temperature increases from 298 K to 308 K, rate constant of a reaction doubles. Activation energy E_a is: (R = 8.314 J/mol·K)", ["52.9 kJ/mol", "105.8 kJ/mol", "26.4 kJ/mol", "211.6 kJ/mol"], 0, "Arrhenius equation: log(k2/k1) = (E_a / 2.303 R) * (T2 - T1) / (T1 T2). log(2) = 0.3010. E_a = (0.3010 * 2.303 * 8.314 * 298 * 308) / 10 = 52.89 kJ/mol.")
add("Chemical Kinetics", "M", "A catalyst increases the rate of a chemical reaction by:", ["Increasing ΔH of reaction", "Decreasing activation energy E_a", "Increasing collision frequency only", "Decreasing free energy change ΔG"], 1, "A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with lower activation energy (E_a).")
add("Chemical Kinetics", "H", "For a pseudo-first order reaction CH3COOCH3 + H2O (excess) -> CH3COOH + CH3OH, order and molecularity are:", ["Order = 1, Molecularity = 2", "Order = 2, Molecularity = 2", "Order = 1, Molecularity = 1", "Order = 2, Molecularity = 1"], 0, "Water is present in large excess, so order = 1. However, 2 molecules participate in elementary step, so molecularity = 2.")
add("Chemical Kinetics", "H", "The plot of ln k versus 1/T yields a straight line with slope equal to:", ["- E_a / R", "E_a / R", "- E_a / (2.303 R)", "E_a / (2.303 R)"], 0, "ln k = ln A - (E_a / R) (1/T). Plot of ln k vs 1/T gives a straight line with slope = - E_a / R.")
add("Chemical Kinetics", "M", "If concentration of reactant in a zero-order reaction is doubled, its half-life period will:", ["Be halved", "Double", "Remain unchanged", "Quadruple"], 1, "For zero-order reaction, t_1/2 = [A]0 / (2 k). Half-life is directly proportional to initial concentration [A]0.")
add("Chemical Kinetics", "H", "Radioactive decay follows kinetics of which order?", ["Zero order", "First order", "Second order", "Fractional order"], 1, "All radioactive decay processes are natural nuclear disintegrations following strictly FIRST-ORDER kinetics.")

# 9. Surface Chemistry (C081-C090)
add("Surface Chemistry", "M", "Which type of adsorption involves weak van der Waals forces and is reversible?", ["Chemisorption", "Physisorption", "Desorption", "Absorption"], 1, "Physisorption (physical adsorption) arises from weak van der Waals forces, is multi-layered, non-specific, and reversible.")
add("Surface Chemistry", "H", "According to Freundlich adsorption isotherm x/m = k P^(1/n), at HIGH pressure, the value of 1/n becomes:", ["1", "0", "0.5", "Infinity"], 1, "At high pressure, adsorption reaches saturation. x/m becomes independent of pressure (P⁰), so 1/n = 0.")
add("Surface Chemistry", "M", "Gold number is a measure of the protective power of a:", ["Lyophilic colloid", "Lyophobic colloid", "Emulsion", "Gel"], 0, "Gold number measures minimum mass in mg of a lyophilic colloid required to prevent coagulation of 10 mL gold sol by 1 mL of 10% NaCl.")
add("Surface Chemistry", "H", "According to Hardy-Schulze rule, coagulation power of ions for a NEGATIVELY charged sol (e.g. As2S3 sol) increases in order:", ["Na⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Al³⁺", "Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Na⁺", "Cl⁻ < SO4²⁻ < PO4³⁻", "PO4³⁻ < SO4²⁻ < Cl⁻"], 0, "For negatively charged sol, coagulating power increases with higher positive charge of flocculating cation: Na⁺ (+1) < Mg²⁺ (+2) < Al³⁺ (+3).")
add("Surface Chemistry", "H", "The charge on Fe(OH)3 colloidal particles formed by adding FeCl3 to hot water is:", ["Positive due to adsorption of Fe³⁺", "Negative due to adsorption of Cl⁻", "Neutral", "Negative due to OH⁻"], 0, "Fe(OH)3 sol preferentially adsorbs common Fe³⁺ ions from solution, acquiring a POSITIVE charge.")
add("Surface Chemistry", "M", "Butter is a colloidal dispersion of:", ["Liquid in liquid", "Liquid in solid", "Solid in liquid", "Gas in liquid"], 1, "Butter is an emulsion/gel composed of liquid water droplets dispersed in solid fat (Liquid in Solid).")
add("Surface Chemistry", "H", "Cleansing action of soap is due to formation of aggregates called micelles above a specific concentration known as:", ["Critical Micelle Concentration (CMC)", "Kraft Temperature", "Isoelectric Point", "Peptization concentration"], 0, "Micelles form only above Critical Micelle Concentration (CMC) and above Kraft Temperature (T_k).")
add("Surface Chemistry", "H", "Tyndall effect is NOT observed in:", ["Starch sol", "Gold sol", "True solution of NaCl in water", "Emulsion of oil in water"], 2, "Tyndall effect (light scattering) occurs only when particle size is comparable to wavelength of light (colloids). True solutions (NaCl in water) do not show Tyndall effect.")
add("Surface Chemistry", "M", "Process of converting a freshly prepared precipitate into a colloidal sol by adding an electrolyte is called:", ["Coagulation", "Peptization", "Dialysis", "Electrophoresis"], 1, "Peptization is the dispersion of a precipitate into colloidal particles by adding a small amount of electrolyte (peptizing agent).")
add("Surface Chemistry", "H", "Electrophoresis confirms that colloidal particles:", ["Possess electrical charge", "Scatter light", "Exhibit Brownian motion", "Have large surface area"], 0, "Electrophoresis (movement of colloidal particles under applied electric field) confirms particles carry an electrical charge.")

# 10. Periodicity (C091-C100)
add("Periodicity", "M", "Which of the following elements has the HIGHEST electronegativity?", ["Oxygen", "Chlorine", "Fluorine", "Nitrogen"], 2, "Fluorine (F) is the most electronegative element on the Pauling scale (electronegativity = 4.0).")
add("Periodicity", "H", "Correct increasing order of ionic radii for isoelectronic species N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺, Mg²⁺ is:", ["Mg²⁺ < Na⁺ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < N³⁻", "N³⁻ < O²⁻ < F⁻ < Na⁺ < Mg²⁺", "Na⁺ < Mg²⁺ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < N³⁻", "N³⁻ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < Mg²⁺ < Na⁺"], 0, "For isoelectronic species, higher nuclear charge (Z) pulls electron cloud closer, reducing radius. Mg²⁺(Z=12) < Na⁺(11) < F⁻(9) < O²⁻(8) < N³⁻(7).")
add("Periodicity", "M", "Element with atomic number Z = 35 belongs to which block and group of Periodic Table?", ["p-block, Group 17", "d-block, Group 7", "s-block, Group 2", "p-block, Group 15"], 0, "Z = 35 is Bromine (Br): configuration [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p⁵. Last electron enters p-subshell (p-block), Group = 10 + 2 + 5 = 17 (Halogens).")
add("Periodicity", "H", "First ionization energy of Nitrogen (N) is HIGHER than Oxygen (O) because:", ["N has smaller atomic radius than O", "N has half-filled 2p³ subshell stability", "O has higher effective nuclear charge", "N is more electronegative than O"], 1, "Nitrogen has electronic configuration [He] 2s² 2p³ with extra stable half-filled 2p subshell, requiring more energy to remove an electron than Oxygen [He] 2s² 2p⁴.")
add("Periodicity", "H", "Which element has the most EXOTHERMIC (highest negative) Electron Gain Enthalpy?", ["Fluorine (F)", "Chlorine (Cl)", "Bromine (Br)", "Oxygen (O)"], 1, "Chlorine (Cl) has more negative electron gain enthalpy than Fluorine (-349 kJ/mol vs -328 kJ/mol) due to small size and high inter-electronic repulsions in F's compact 2p subshell.")
add("Periodicity", "M", "General valence shell electronic configuration of d-block elements is:", ["(n-1)d¹⁻¹⁰ ns¹⁻²", "(n-1)d¹⁻¹⁰ ns² np⁶", "nd¹⁻¹⁰ ns¹", "(n-2)f¹⁻¹⁴ (n-1)d⁰⁻¹ ns²"], 0, "Transition elements (d-block) have outer electronic configuration (n-1)d¹⁻¹⁰ ns¹⁻².")
add("Periodicity", "H", "Which of the following oxides is AMPHOTERIC in nature?", ["Na2O", "Cl2O7", "Al2O3", "SO3"], 2, "Al2O3 and BeO are amphoteric oxides, reacting with both acids and bases. Na2O is basic; Cl2O7 and SO3 are acidic.")
add("Periodicity", "H", "Screening / shielding effect order of subshell orbitals for a given principal quantum number is:", ["s > p > d > f", "f > d > p > s", "s > d > p > f", "p > s > d > f"], 0, "s-orbitals penetrate closest to the nucleus and exert the strongest screening effect: s > p > d > f.")
add("Periodicity", "M", "Diagonal relationship in Periodic Table is shown by elements of 2nd and 3rd periods, such as:", ["Li and Mg", "Be and Al", "B and Si", "All of the above"], 3, "Diagonal pairs include Li-Mg, Be-Al, B-Si due to similar ionic potential (charge-to-radius ratio).")
add("Periodicity", "H", "The IUPAC symbol for element with atomic number Z = 114 is:", ["Ununquadium (Uuq)", "Ununbium (Uub)", "Ununtrium (Uut)", "Ununpentium (Uup)"], 0, "1 = un, 1 = un, 4 = quad => Un-un-quad-ium (Uuq). (Now officially named Flerovium Fl).")

# 11. Chemical Bonding & Molecular Structure (C101-C110)
add("Chemical Bonding & Molecular Structure", "M", "According to VSEPR theory, geometry and hybridization of XeF4 molecule are:", ["Square planar, sp³d²", "Tetrahedral, sp³", "Octahedral, sp³d²", "See-saw, sp³d"], 0, "XeF4 has 4 bonding pairs and 2 lone pairs on Xe (total 6 electron pairs => sp³d² hybridization, Octahedral geometry, Square Planar molecular shape).")
add("Chemical Bonding & Molecular Structure", "H", "Bond order of O2⁺, O2, O2⁻, O2²⁻ species follow the decreasing order:", ["O2⁺ > O2 > O2⁻ > O2²⁻", "O2²⁻ > O2⁻ > O2 > O2⁺", "O2 > O2⁺ > O2⁻ > O2²⁻", "O2⁺ > O2⁻ > O2 > O2²⁻"], 0, "MO theory bond orders: O2⁺ = 2.5, O2 = 2.0, O2⁻ = 1.5, O2²⁻ = 1.0. Decreasing order: O2⁺ > O2 > O2⁻ > O2²⁻.")
add("Chemical Bonding & Molecular Structure", "M", "Which of the following molecules has ZERO dipole moment?", ["H2O", "NH3", "BF3", "PCl3"], 2, "BF3 is trigonal planar (sp² hybridization). The three vector dipole moments cancel completely, yielding net dipole moment μ = 0.")
add("Chemical Bonding & Molecular Structure", "H", "According to Molecular Orbital Theory, which of the following diatomic species is PARAMAGNETIC?", ["N2", "O2", "C2", "F2"], 1, "O2 has 16 electrons with two unpaired electrons in degenerate π*2px and π*2py antibonding orbitals, making it paramagnetic.")
add("Chemical Bonding & Molecular Structure", "H", "Hybridization of central atom in PCl5, SF6, and IF7 are respectively:", ["sp³d, sp³d², sp³d³", "sp³d², sp³d, sp³d³", "sp³, sp³d, sp³d²", "sp³d³, sp³d², sp³d"], 0, "PCl5 (5 BP, 0 LP) = sp³d. SF6 (6 BP, 0 LP) = sp³d². IF7 (7 BP, 0 LP) = sp³d³.")
add("Chemical Bonding & Molecular Structure", "M", "The bond angle H-O-H in water molecule (104.5°) is smaller than ideal tetrahedral angle (109.5°) due to:", ["Lone pair - Lone pair repulsion", "Bond pair - Bond pair repulsion", "Lone pair - Bond pair attraction", "High electronegativity of H"], 0, "Repulsion order: Lone Pair - Lone Pair > Lone Pair - Bond Pair > Bond Pair - Bond Pair. Oxygen's 2 LPs push the 2 BPs closer.")
add("Chemical Bonding & Molecular Structure", "H", "In NO3⁻ ion, total number of valence electrons, formal charge on Nitrogen, and resonance structures are:", ["24 electrons, +1 charge, 3 structures", "24 electrons, 0 charge, 2 structures", "23 electrons, +1 charge, 3 structures", "26 electrons, -1 charge, 4 structures"], 0, "Valence electrons = 5 + 3(6) + 1 = 24. N forms 1 double bond, 2 single bonds (formal charge = 5 - 4 = +1). 3 equivalent resonance structures.")
add("Chemical Bonding & Molecular Structure", "H", "Which of the following compounds exhibits intramolecular hydrogen bonding?", ["o-Nitrophenol", "p-Nitrophenol", "Water", "Ethanol"], 0, "o-Nitrophenol forms 6-membered ring intramolecular hydrogen bond between phenolic -OH and adjacent ortho -NO2 group.")
add("Chemical Bonding & Molecular Structure", "M", "According to Fajan's rules, covalent character in an ionic compound increases with:", ["Small cation, large anion, high charge", "Large cation, small anion, low charge", "Small cation, small anion, low charge", "Large cation, large anion, low charge"], 0, "High polarizing power of small cation with high positive charge + high polarizability of large anion = high covalent character.")
add("Chemical Bonding & Molecular Structure", "H", "Number of sigma (σ) and pi (π) bonds in a molecule of Ethyne (Acetylene, C2H2) are:", ["3 σ and 2 π", "2 σ and 3 π", "4 σ and 1 π", "5 σ and 0 π"], 0, "Ethyne H-C≡C-H contains two C-H σ bonds, one C-C σ bond (total 3 σ bonds), and two C-C π bonds.")

# 12. s-Block Elements (C111-C120)
add("s-Block Elements", "M", "Which alkali metal imparts a characteristic CRIMSON RED color to Bunsen flame?", ["Sodium (Na)", "Lithium (Li)", "Potassium (K)", "Cesium (Cs)"], 1, "Flame colors: Li (Crimson Red), Na (Golden Yellow), K (Lilac/Violet), Rb (Red-Violet), Cs (Blue).")
add("s-Block Elements", "H", "Solubility of alkaline earth metal sulfates in water decreases down the group in order BeSO4 > MgSO4 > CaSO4 > SrSO4 > BaSO4 because:", ["Hydration energy decreases faster than lattice energy", "Lattice energy increases rapidly", "Atomic size decreases", "Electronegativity increases"], 0, "As cation size increases, hydration enthalpy decreases much more rapidly than lattice enthalpy, making BaSO4 insoluble.")
add("s-Block Elements", "M", "Plaster of Paris has the chemical formula:", ["CaSO4 · 2 H2O", "CaSO4 · 0.5 H2O", "CaSO4", "MgSO4 · 7 H2O"], 1, "Plaster of Paris is Calcium Sulfate Hemihydrate: CaSO4 · 0.5 H2O (or (CaSO4)2 · H2O). Gypsum is CaSO4 · 2 H2O.")
add("s-Block Elements", "H", "When Sodium metal dissolves in liquid Ammonia, a deep blue conducting solution is formed due to presence of:", ["Ammoniated electrons", "Sodium cations Na⁺", "Ammoniated Na⁺ ions", "Ammonia gas"], 0, "Na + (x+y) NH3 → [Na(NH3)_x]⁺ + [e(NH3)_y]⁻. The deep blue color and paramagnetic nature are due to ammoniated electrons.")
add("s-Block Elements", "H", "Which of the following alkali metal carbonates is thermally UNSTABLE and decomposes on heating to yield CO2 gas?", ["Na2CO3", "K2CO3", "Li2CO3", "Rb2CO3"], 2, "Li2CO3 is thermally unstable due to small size and high polarizing power of Li⁺ ion: Li2CO3 → Li2O + CO2. Other alkali carbonates are stable.")
add("s-Block Elements", "M", "Chemical formula of Quicklime and Slaked lime are respectively:", ["CaO and Ca(OH)2", "CaCO3 and CaO", "Ca(OH)2 and CaO", "CaCl2 and CaSO4"], 0, "Quicklime is Calcium Oxide (CaO). Slaked lime is Calcium Hydroxide Ca(OH)2.")
add("s-Block Elements", "H", "Magnesium burns in air to form a mixture of:", ["MgO and Mg(NO3)2", "MgO and Mg3N2", "MgCO3 and MgO", "Mg(OH)2 and Mg3N2"], 1, "Unlike group 1 elements, Magnesium reacts with both Oxygen and Nitrogen in air to form Oxide (MgO) and Nitride (Mg3N2).")
add("s-Block Elements", "H", "Biological importance of Na⁺ and K⁺ ions: Na⁺ ions are primarily found in:", ["Extracellular fluid, while K⁺ ions are predominant in intracellular fluid", "Intracellular fluid, while K⁺ is extracellular", "Bone matrix only", "Red blood cells only"], 0, "Na⁺ is the primary cation in extracellular fluids (plasma, interstitial fluid), whereas K⁺ is the main intracellular cation.")
add("s-Block Elements", "M", "Solvay process is commercially used for the manufacture of:", ["Sodium Carbonate (Washing Soda)", "Sodium Hydroxide (Caustic Soda)", "Sodium Nitrate", "Potassium Carbonate"], 0, "Solvay (Ammonia-Soda) process manufactures Sodium Carbonate (Na2CO3). Note K2CO3 cannot be prepared by Solvay process due to high solubility of KHCO3.")
add("s-Block Elements", "H", "Which alkaline earth metal does NOT impart any color to Bunsen flame?", ["Calcium (Ca)", "Strontium (Sr)", "Beryllium (Be) and Magnesium (Mg)", "Barium (Ba)"], 2, "Be and Mg have small size and very high ionization energies; electrons are tightly held and not excited by Bunsen flame.")

# 13. p-Block Elements (C121-C130)
add("p-Block Elements", "M", "Inert pair effect is most prominent in heavier elements of groups 13, 14, 15, resulting in stability of lower oxidation state in:", ["Tl⁺, Pb²⁺, Bi³⁺", "Al³⁺, Si⁴⁺, P⁵⁺", "B³⁺, C⁴⁺, N⁵⁺", "Ga³⁺, Ge⁴⁺, As³⁺"], 0, "Inert pair effect (reluctance of s-electrons to participate in bonding) stabilizes lower oxidation states: Tl⁺ > Tl³⁺, Pb²⁺ > Pb⁴⁺, Bi³⁺ > Bi⁵⁺.")
add("p-Block Elements", "H", "Structure of Diborane (B2H6) contains:", ["Four 2-center 2-electron bonds and two 3-center 2-electron bridge bonds", "Six 2-center 2-electron bonds", "Two 3-center 4-electron bonds", "Four 3-center 2-electron bonds"], 0, "B2H6 has 4 terminal B-H bonds (2c-2e bonds) and 2 banana bridge B-H-B bonds (3c-2e bonds).")
add("p-Block Elements", "M", "Which allotrope of Carbon is thermodynamically MOST stable at room temperature and pressure?", ["Diamond", "Graphite", "Fullerene (C60)", "Carbon Nanotubes"], 1, "Graphite is the thermodynamically most stable allotrope of carbon (ΔH°_f of graphite = 0, diamond = +1.9 kJ/mol).")
add("p-Block Elements", "H", "When white Phosphorus is heated with concentrated NaOH solution in an inert CO2 atmosphere, gas evolved is:", ["PH3 (Phosphine)", "P2H4", "H3PO4", "P2O5"], 0, "Disproportionation reaction: P4 + 3 NaOH + 3 H2O → PH3(g) + 3 NaH2PO2 (Sodium hypophosphite). Phosphine gas PH3 is liberated.")
add("p-Block Elements", "H", "In Contact Process for manufacture of H2SO4, catalyst used for oxidation of SO2 to SO3 is:", ["V2O5 (Vanadium pentoxide)", "Fe2O3", "Pt / Rh gauge", "CuCl2"], 0, "Vanadium pentoxide V2O5 (or Pt) catalyzes 2 SO2 + O2 ⇌ 2 SO3.")
add("p-Block Elements", "M", "Which halogen oxoacid is the STRONGEST acid?", ["HClO", "HClO2", "HClO3", "HClO4"], 3, "Acidity of halogen oxoacids increases with increasing oxidation state of halogen: HClO (+1) < HClO2 (+3) < HClO3 (+5) < HClO4 (+7).")
add("p-Block Elements", "H", "Noble gas compound XeF6 on complete hydrolysis yields:", ["XeO3 + HF", "XeO2F2 + HF", "XeOF4 + HF", "Xe + O2 + HF"], 0, "XeF6 + 3 H2O → XeO3 + 6 HF. Xenon trioxide XeO3 is an explosive solid.")
add("p-Block Elements", "H", "Borax bead test involves heating Borax (Na2B4O7 · 10H2O) to form a transparent glassy bead composed of:", ["NaBO2 + B2O3", "H3BO3", "Na2B4O7", "B2O3 only"], 0, "Na2B4O7 · 10H2O --Δ--> Na2B4O7 --Δ--> 2 NaBO2 (Sodium metaborate) + B2O3 (Boric anhydride).")
add("p-Block Elements", "M", "Nitrogen molecule (N2) is chemically unreactive at room temperature because of:", ["High electronegativity of N", "Presence of strong triple bond (N≡N) with high dissociation energy", "Small atomic size", "Absence of d-orbitals"], 1, "N2 possesses a triple bond (N≡N) with extremely high bond dissociation enthalpy (945 kJ/mol).")
add("p-Block Elements", "H", "Which of the following compounds is known as 'Inorganic Benzene'?", ["Borazine (B3N3H6)", "Diborane (B2H6)", "Phosphazene", "Boron nitride (BN)"], 0, "Borazine B3N3H6 is called inorganic benzene due to its cyclic planar structure, alternating B-N atoms, and aromatic electron delocalization.")

# 14. d- and f-Block Elements (C131-C140)
add("d- and f-Block Elements", "M", "Which transition metal ion is COLORLESS in aqueous solution?", ["Cu²⁺", "Fe³⁺", "Zn²⁺", "Cr³⁺"], 2, "Zn²⁺ has electronic configuration [Ar] 3d¹⁰ (fully filled d-subshell). Absence of unpaired d-electrons means no d-d transition, making it colorless.")
add("d- and f-Block Elements", "H", "Spin-only magnetic moment of Mn²⁺ ion (Z = 25) is: (in Bohr Magnetons, BM)", ["5.92 BM", "4.90 BM", "3.87 BM", "2.83 BM"], 0, "Mn²⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁵ (n = 5 unpaired electrons). μ = √(n(n+2)) = √(5 * 7) = √35 ≈ 5.92 BM.")
add("d- and f-Block Elements", "M", "Lanthanide contraction is caused by poor shielding effect of:", ["4f electrons", "5d electrons", "6s electrons", "3d electrons"], 0, "Poor shielding effect of diffuse 4f electrons causes effective nuclear charge to increase steadily across lanthanides, shrinking atomic/ionic radii.")
add("d- and f-Block Elements", "H", "Potassium Permanganate (KMnO4) acts as an oxidizing agent in acidic medium. Change in oxidation state of Mn is:", ["+7 to +2 (gains 5 e-)", "+7 to +4 (gains 3 e-)", "+7 to +6 (gains 1 e-)", "+6 to +2 (gains 4 e-)"], 0, "In acidic medium: MnO4⁻ + 8 H⁺ + 5 e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4 H2O (Mn⁺⁷ → Mn⁺²).")
add("d- and f-Block Elements", "H", "Which pair of transition metals exhibit almost identical ionic radii due to Lanthanide Contraction?", ["Zr (Zr⁴⁺) and Hf (Hf⁴⁺)", "Ti and Zr", "Fe and Co", "Cu and Ag"], 0, "Zr (4d series, 160 pm) and Hf (5d series, 159 pm) have nearly identical radii due to intervention of 14 lanthanides.")
add("d- and f-Block Elements", "M", "Transition elements form interstitial compounds with small non-metal atoms like H, C, N. These compounds are:", ["Hard, metallic, high melting point", "Soft and ionic", "Volatile and low melting", "Chemically highly reactive"], 0, "Interstitial compounds retain metallic conductivity, are extremely hard (near diamond hardness), and have very high melting points.")
add("d- and f-Block Elements", "H", "When K2Cr2O7 is reacted with H2O2 in acidic medium in presence of ether, a deep blue color is produced due to formation of:", ["CrO5 (Chromium peroxide)", "Cr3+ ions", "CrO4²⁻", "Cr2O3"], 0, "Cr2O7²⁻ + 4 H2O2 + 2 H⁺ → 2 CrO5 (butterfly structure Chromium peroxide) + 5 H2O. Ether stabilizes blue CrO5.")
add("d- and f-Block Elements", "H", "Highest oxidation state shown by an actinide element is +7, exhibited by:", ["Neptunium (Np) and Plutonium (Pu)", "Uranium (U)", "Thorium (Th)", "Americium (Am)"], 0, "Np and Pu exhibit maximum oxidation state of +7 in actinide series.")
add("d- and f-Block Elements", "M", "Which transition metal catalyst is used in Haber's Process for ammonia production?", ["Finely divided Iron (Fe) with Mo promoter", "Nickel (Ni)", "Platinum (Pt)", "V2O5"], 0, "Haber process utilizes finely divided Iron (Fe) catalyst with Molybdenum (Mo) as promoter.")
add("d- and f-Block Elements", "H", "Paramagnetism in transition metal complexes is caused by presence of:", ["Unpaired electrons", "Paired electrons", "High lattice energy", "empty d-orbitals"], 0, "Paramagnetism arises from magnetic moments of unpaired electrons.")

# 15. Coordination Compounds (C141-C150)
add("Coordination Compounds", "M", "IUPAC name of complex [Co(NH3)5(CO3)]Cl is:", ["Pentaamminecarbonatocobalt(III) chloride", "Pentaamminecarbonatocobalt(II) chloride", "Carbonatopentaamminecobalt(III) chloride", "Pentaamminecobalt(III) carbonate"], 0, "Ligands in alphabetical order: pentaammine + carbonato. Oxidation state of Co: x + 0 + (-2) + (-1) = 0 => x = +3. Pentaamminecarbonatocobalt(III) chloride.")
add("Coordination Compounds", "H", "According to Crystal Field Theory, for an octahedral d⁶ complex in STRONG field ligand environment (Δo > P), the electronic configuration is:", ["t2g⁶ eg⁰ (low spin, diamagnetic)", "t2g⁴ eg² (high spin)", "t2g⁵ eg¹", "t2g³ eg³"], 0, "In strong ligand field (Δo > P), pairing is favored. All 6 d-electrons pair up in lower t2g set: t2g⁶ eg⁰ (Low Spin, diamagnetic).")
add("Coordination Compounds", "M", "Which type of isomerism is exhibited by pair [Co(NH3)5(SO4)]Br and [Co(NH3)5Br]SO4?", ["Ionization isomerism", "Linkage isomerism", "Coordination isomerism", "Solvate isomerism"], 0, "They yield different ions in solution (Br⁻ vs SO4²⁻), exhibiting Ionization Isomerism.")
add("Coordination Compounds", "H", "Number of geometrical isomers possible for square planar complex [Pt(NH3)2Cl2] (Cisplatin) is:", ["2 (Cis and Trans)", "3", "4", "None"], 0, "Square planar [MA2B2] complexes form 2 geometrical isomers: Cis-isomer (Cisplatin, anti-cancer drug) and Trans-isomer.")
add("Coordination Compounds", "H", "Which ligand can act as an AMBIDENTATE ligand?", ["NO2⁻ (or SCN⁻)", "NH3", "H2O", "ethylenediamine (en)"], 0, "Ambidentate ligands possess two donor atoms but coordinate through only one at a time (e.g. NO2⁻ via N or O; SCN⁻ via S or N).")
